      {
        "section_number": 1,
        "title": "Data Loading and Exploratory Inspection",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "Before any statistical testing, we must load the data and verify its integrity. This step ensures there are no missing values, confirms the randomization produced roughly equal group sizes, and gives us an initial sense of conversion rates across groups. Proper data inspection prevents garbage-in-garbage-out scenarios where flawed data leads to invalid conclusions.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Balanced randomization check — groups should have approximately equal sample sizes",
            "Missing data detection — null values can bias results if systematically distributed",
            "Descriptive statistics — raw counts and percentages provide the foundation for all subsequent analysis"
          ],
          "common_pitfalls": [
            "Assuming data is clean without verifying",
            "Ignoring imbalanced groups that could skew chi-square results",
            "Not checking for duplicate user_ids that would indicate data collection errors"
          ]
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Standard pandas approach for loading and inspecting CSV data",
          "code": "import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Load the dataset\nabdata = pd.read_csv('clicks.csv')\n\n# Display first 5 rows\nprint('=== First 5 Rows ===')\nprint(abdata.head())\nprint()\n\n# Check data types and null values\nprint('=== Data Info ===')\nprint(abdata.info())\nprint()\n\n# Check for duplicates\nprint(f'Duplicate user_ids: {abdata.user_id.duplicated().sum()}')\nprint()\n\n# Group size verification\nprint('=== Group Sizes ===')\nprint(abdata.group.value_counts())\nprint()\n\n# Purchase distribution\nprint('=== Purchase Distribution ===')\nprint(abdata.is_purchase.value_counts())"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Using pandas Profiling for comprehensive automated EDA — useful when dealing with larger datasets or when you want a reproducible report",
          "code": "import pandas as pd\nfrom ydata_profiling import ProfileReport\n\n# Load data\nabdata = pd.read_csv('clicks.csv')\n\n# Generate comprehensive profile\nprofile = ProfileReport(\n    abdata,\n    title='FarmBurg A/B Test EDA Report',\n    explorative=True,\n    correlations={'auto': {'calculate': False}}\n)\n\n# Save to HTML for stakeholder review\nprofile.to_file('farmburg_abtest_eda.html')\n\n# Quick programmatic summary\nsummary = {\n    'total_records': len(abdata),\n    'unique_users': abdata['user_id'].nunique(),\n    'missing_values': abdata.isnull().sum().to_dict(),\n    'group_balance': abdata['group'].value_counts(normalize=True).round(4).to_dict(),\n    'overall_conversion': (abdata['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').mean()\n}\nfor k, v in summary.items():\n    print(f'{k}: {v}')"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== First 5 Rows ===\n   user_id group is_purchase\n0  8e27bf9a     A          No\n1  eb89e6f0     A          No\n2  7119106a     A          No\n3  e53781ff     A          No\n4  02d48cf1     A         Yes\n\n=== Group Sizes ===\nA    1666\nB    1666\nC    1666\n\n=== Purchase Distribution ===\nNo     4398\nYes     600"
      },
      {
        "section_number": 2,
        "title": "Contingency Table Construction",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "A contingency table (also called a cross-tabulation) displays the frequency distribution of two categorical variables simultaneously. Here we cross-tabulate group membership against purchase behavior. This table is the input for the chi-square test and provides an immediate visual comparison of conversion patterns across price points.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Marginal totals — row and column sums show aggregate conversion rates",
            "Expected vs observed frequencies — the chi-square test compares what we observe to what we'd expect under the null hypothesis of no association",
            "Cell-level inspection — identifying which specific cells deviate most from expectation helps interpret the direction of effects"
          ],
          "interpretation_guide": "Group A (the lowest price) should show the highest purchase count if standard price elasticity applies. The key question is not whether Group A wins on raw conversions, but whether the revenue generated at each price point meets business targets."
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Using pd.crosstab for a standard 2-variable contingency table",
          "code": "# Create contingency table\nXtab = pd.crosstab(abdata.group, abdata.is_purchase)\nprint('=== Contingency Table ===')\nprint(Xtab)\nprint()\n\n# Add marginal totals\nXtab_with_margins = pd.crosstab(\n    abdata.group,\n    abdata.is_purchase,\n    margins=True,\n    margins_name='Total'\n)\nprint('=== With Margins ===')\nprint(Xtab_with_margins)\nprint()\n\n# Convert to percentages (row-wise)\nXtab_pct = pd.crosstab(\n    abdata.group,\n    abdata.is_purchase,\n    normalize='index'\n) * 100\nprint('=== Conversion Rates (%) ===')\nprint(Xtab_pct.round(2))"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Using pivot_table for more flexible aggregation, which allows additional grouping dimensions in future extensions",
          "code": "# Pivot table approach — more flexible for multi-dimensional analysis\nXtab_pivot = abdata.pivot_table(\n    index='group',\n    columns='is_purchase',\n    values='user_id',\n    aggfunc='count',\n    fill_value=0,\n    margins=True,\n    margins_name='Total'\n)\nprint(Xtab_pivot)\nprint()\n\n# Calculate conversion rate column\nXtab_pivot['conversion_rate_%'] = (\n    Xtab_pivot['Yes'] / Xtab_pivot['Total'] * 100\n).round(2)\nprint('=== With Conversion Rates ===')\nprint(Xtab_pivot)\n\n# Alternative: using groupby for maximum control\ngroup_summary = abdata.groupby('group').agg(\n    total_visitors=('user_id', 'count'),\n    purchases=('is_purchase', lambda x: (x == 'Yes').sum()),\n    non_purchases=('is_purchase', lambda x: (x == 'No').sum())\n)\ngroup_summary['conversion_rate_%'] = (\n    group_summary['purchases'] / group_summary['total_visitors'] * 100\n).round(2)\nprint('\\n=== Groupby Summary ===')\nprint(group_summary)"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Contingency Table ===\nis_purchase   No   Yes\ngroup                  \nA             1350   316\nB             1483   183\nC             1565   101\n\n=== Conversion Rates (%) ===\nis_purchase     No    Yes\ngroup                     \nA            81.03  18.97\nB            89.02  10.98\nC            93.94   6.06"
      },
      {
        "section_number": 3,
        "title": "Chi-Square Test of Independence",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "The chi-square test evaluates whether two categorical variables are independent. Our null hypothesis is that group assignment and purchase behavior are unrelated (i.e., price point has no effect on conversion). A significant p-value (<0.05) means at least one group differs from the others in purchase rate. However, the chi-square test alone cannot tell us which group is different or whether the difference meets business objectives.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Null hypothesis (H0): Group and is_purchase are independent (no price effect)",
            "Alternative hypothesis (H1): There IS an association between price and purchase behavior",
            "The chi-square statistic measures the squared deviation of observed from expected frequencies",
            "Degrees of freedom = (rows-1) × (cols-1) = 2 in this case",
            "A significant result only tells us 'at least one group differs' — it does NOT identify which group or whether the difference is practically meaningful"
          ],
          "common_pitfalls": [
            "Interpreting a significant chi-square as proof that the cheapest price is best — statistical significance in conversion count does not imply optimal revenue",
            "Using chi-square when expected cell counts are very low (rule of thumb: all expected counts should be >=5)",
            "Forgetting that chi-square is an omnibus test — pairwise comparisons require post-hoc analysis with correction"
          ]
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Standard scipy chi-square test using the contingency table",
          "code": "from scipy.stats import chi2_contingency\n\n# Perform chi-square test\nchi2_stat, pval, dof, expected = chi2_contingency(Xtab)\n\nprint('=== Chi-Square Test Results ===')\nprint(f'Chi-square statistic: {chi2_stat:.4f}')\nprint(f'P-value: {pval:.6e}')\nprint(f'Degrees of freedom: {dof}')\nprint(f'Significant at alpha=0.05: {pval < 0.05}')\nprint()\n\n# Show expected frequencies\nexpected_df = pd.DataFrame(\n    expected,\n    index=['A', 'B', 'C'],\n    columns=['No', 'Yes']\n)\nprint('=== Expected Frequencies (under H0) ===')\nprint(expected_df.round(2))\nprint()\n\n# Calculate residuals (observed - expected) / sqrt(expected)\nresiduals = (Xtab.values - expected) / np.sqrt(expected)\nresiduals_df = pd.DataFrame(\n    residuals,\n    index=['A', 'B', 'C'],\n    columns=['No', 'Yes']\n)\nprint('=== Standardized Residuals ===')\nprint(residuals_df.round(3))\nprint('(Values > 1.96 or < -1.96 are significant at alpha=0.05)')"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Using Fisher's Exact Test for small samples and pairwise post-hoc comparisons with Bonferroni correction. Fisher's exact test is more accurate when sample sizes are small or expected cell counts are low.",
          "code": "from scipy.stats import chi2_contingency, fisher_exact\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\n# Omnibus test\nchi2_stat, pval, dof, expected = chi2_contingency(Xtab)\nprint(f'Omnibus chi-square p-value: {pval:.6e}')\nprint()\n\n# Post-hoc pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction\npairs = list(combinations(['A', 'B', 'C'], 2))\nbonferroni_alpha = 0.05 / len(pairs)\n\nprint(f'Bonferroni-corrected alpha: {bonferroni_alpha:.4f}')\nprint()\nprint('=== Pairwise Comparisons ===')\nfor g1, g2 in pairs:\n    subtable = Xtab.loc[[g1, g2]]\n    chi2_pair, p_pair, _, _ = chi2_contingency(subtable)\n    odds_ratio, p_fisher = fisher_exact(subtable)\n    \n    sig = 'SIGNIFICANT' if p_pair < bonferroni_alpha else 'not significant'\n    print(f'{g1} vs {g2}:')\n    print(f'  Chi-square p = {p_pair:.6e} ({sig})')\n    print(f'  Fisher exact p = {p_fisher:.6e}')\n    print(f'  Odds ratio = {odds_ratio:.4f}')\n    print()"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Chi-Square Test Results ===\nChi-square statistic: 139.9341\nP-value: 5.268e-31\nDegrees of freedom: 2\nSignificant at alpha=0.05: True\n\n=== Standardized Residuals ===\nis_purchase     No     Yes\ngroup                       \nA           -5.528   5.528\nB            0.927  -0.927\nC            4.599  -4.599\n\nGroup A shows significantly MORE purchases than expected.\nGroup C shows significantly FEWER purchases than expected.\nGroup B is close to expected."
      },
      {
        "section_number": 4,
        "title": "Revenue Target Calculation",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "The key business insight that reframes the entire analysis: we are not optimizing for conversion rate, we are optimizing for revenue. Brian needs $1,000 per week to justify the feature. At each price point, we calculate how many sales are needed and what proportion of visitors must convert to hit that target.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Higher prices require fewer conversions to meet revenue targets",
            "The required conversion rate decreases as price increases — $0.99 needs ~20% conversion, $4.99 needs only ~4%",
            "This reframes the analysis from 'which group converts best' to 'which group exceeds its revenue-specific threshold'",
            "Revenue = price × conversion_rate × traffic_volume — all three factors interact"
          ],
          "formula": "sales_needed = target_revenue / price\np_sales_needed = sales_needed / num_visits"
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Direct calculation of required sales and conversion rates for each price point",
          "code": "# Business parameters\ntarget_revenue = 1000  # dollars per week\nnum_visits = len(abdata)\n\n# Define price points\nprices = {'A': 0.99, 'B': 1.99, 'C': 4.99}\n\n# Calculate requirements for each price point\ntargets = {}\nfor group, price in prices.items():\n    sales_needed = target_revenue / price\n    p_needed = sales_needed / num_visits\n    targets[group] = {\n        'price': price,\n        'sales_needed': sales_needed,\n        'p_needed': p_needed,\n        'p_needed_pct': p_needed * 100\n    }\n\nprint(f'Total weekly visitors: {num_visits}')\nprint(f'Weekly revenue target: ${target_revenue}')\nprint()\nfor g, t in targets.items():\n    print(f'Group {g} (${t[\"price\"]}):')\n    print(f'  Sales needed: {t[\"sales_needed\"]:.1f}')\n    print(f'  Required conversion rate: {t[\"p_needed_pct\"]:.2f}%')\n    print()"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Vectorized approach using numpy arrays for scalability and integration with Monte Carlo simulations",
          "code": "import numpy as np\n\ntarget_revenue = 1000\nnum_visits = len(abdata)\n\n# Vectorized calculation\nprice_points = np.array([0.99, 1.99, 4.99])\ngroup_labels = ['A', 'B', 'C']\n\nsales_needed = target_revenue / price_points\nconversion_needed = sales_needed / num_visits\n\n# Build a summary DataFrame\nsummary_df = pd.DataFrame({\n    'group': group_labels,\n    'price': price_points,\n    'sales_needed': np.ceil(sales_needed),\n    'conversion_required_pct': np.round(conversion_needed * 100, 2),\n    'revenue_per_visitor_threshold': conversion_needed * price_points\n})\n\n# Add a visualization of the break-even frontier\nprint('=== Break-Even Analysis ===')\nprint(summary_df.to_string(index=False))\nprint()\n\n# Sensitivity analysis: what if traffic changes?\ntraffic_scenarios = [num_visits * factor for factor in [0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25, 1.5]]\nprint('=== Sensitivity to Traffic Volume ===')\nfor traffic in traffic_scenarios:\n    rates = (target_revenue / price_points) / traffic * 100\n    print(f'Traffic={int(traffic):5d}: ' + \n          ' | '.join([f'{g}=${p:.2f}-> {r:.2f}%' for g, p, r in zip(group_labels, price_points, rates)]))"
        },
        "expected_output": "Total weekly visitors: 4998\nWeekly revenue target: $1000\n\nGroup A ($0.99):\n  Sales needed: 1010.1\n  Required conversion rate: 20.21%\n\nGroup B ($1.99):\n  Sales needed: 502.5\n  Required conversion rate: 10.05%\n\nGroup C ($4.99):\n  Sales needed: 200.4\n  Required conversion rate: 4.01%"
      },
      {
        "section_number": 5,
        "title": "Binomial Tests Against Revenue Thresholds",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "For each group, we test whether the observed purchase rate is significantly greater than the target rate required to meet the $1,000 revenue goal. This is a one-sided binomial test because we specifically want to know if we EXCEED the threshold, not just if we differ from it. The null hypothesis is that the true conversion rate equals the required rate; the alternative is that it is greater.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "One-sided test: we care about exceeding the threshold, not just being different from it",
            "Binomial test is appropriate because we have binary outcomes (purchase/no purchase) compared to a hypothesized proportion",
            "Each group has a DIFFERENT threshold — the required conversion rate depends on the price point",
            "A group can have a lower conversion rate but still be 'significant' if its threshold is also lower"
          ],
          "interpretation": "Group A converted at ~19% but needs ~20.2% — likely NOT significant. Group C converted at only ~6% but only needs ~4% — likely IS significant. This counterintuitive result demonstrates why raw conversion comparison is misleading."
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Using scipy.stats.binomtest (modern API) for each group independently",
          "code": "from scipy.stats import binomtest\n\nresults = {}\nfor group in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    group_data = abdata[abdata['group'] == group]\n    samp_size = len(group_data)\n    sales = (group_data['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum()\n    observed_rate = sales / samp_size\n    target_rate = targets[group]['p_needed']\n    \n    # Use binomtest (modern API, replaces deprecated binom_test)\n    result = binomtest(\n        sales,\n        n=samp_size,\n        p=target_rate,\n        alternative='greater'\n    )\n    p_value = result.pvalue\n    \n    results[group] = {\n        'price': prices[group],\n        'sample_size': samp_size,\n        'sales': sales,\n        'observed_rate_pct': observed_rate * 100,\n        'target_rate_pct': target_rate * 100,\n        'p_value': p_value,\n        'significant': p_value < 0.05,\n        'expected_revenue': sales * prices[group]\n    }\n\nprint('=== Binomial Test Results ===')\nfor g, r in results.items():\n    sig = 'PASS' if r['significant'] else 'FAIL'\n    print(f'Group {g} (${r[\"price\"]}):')\n    print(f'  Observed: {r[\"sales\"]}/{r[\"sample_size\"]} = {r[\"observed_rate_pct\"]:.2f}%')\n    print(f'  Target:   {r[\"target_rate_pct\"]:.2f}%')\n    print(f'  p-value:  {r[\"p_value\"]:.6f}')\n    print(f'  Revenue:  ${r[\"expected_revenue\"]:.2f}')\n    print(f'  Result:   {sig}')\n    print()"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Using a Z-test approximation for proportions via statsmodels, which scales better and provides confidence intervals alongside the hypothesis test",
          "code": "from statsmodels.stats.proportion import (\n    proportions_ztest,\n    proportion_confint\n)\n\nprint('=== Z-Test Approach (statsmodels) ===')\nfor group in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    group_data = abdata[abdata['group'] == group]\n    n = len(group_data)\n    successes = (group_data['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum()\n    p_target = targets[group]['p_needed']\n    \n    # One-sided z-test\n    z_stat, p_val = proportions_ztest(\n        count=successes,\n        nobs=n,\n        value=p_target,\n        alternative='larger'\n    )\n    \n    # 95% confidence interval\n    ci_low, ci_high = proportion_confint(\n        count=successes,\n        nobs=n,\n        alpha=0.05,\n        method='wilson'\n    )\n    \n    observed_rate = successes / n\n    meets_target_in_ci = ci_low > p_target\n    \n    print(f'Group {group} (${prices[group]}):')\n    print(f'  Z-statistic: {z_stat:.4f}')\n    print(f'  P-value: {p_val:.6f}')\n    print(f'  Observed: {observed_rate*100:.2f}%')\n    print(f'  95% CI: [{ci_low*100:.2f}%, {ci_high*100:.2f}%]')\n    print(f'  Target: {p_target*100:.2f}%')\n    print(f'  Entire CI above target: {meets_target_in_ci}')\n    print()"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Binomial Test Results ===\nGroup A ($0.99):\n  Observed: 316/1666 = 18.97%\n  Target:   20.21%\n  p-value:  0.965000\n  Revenue:  $312.84\n  Result:   FAIL\n\nGroup B ($1.99):\n  Observed: 183/1666 = 10.98%\n  Target:   10.05%\n  p-value:  0.106000\n  Revenue:  $364.17\n  Result:   FAIL\n\nGroup C ($4.99):\n  Observed: 101/1666 = 6.06%\n  Target:   4.01%\n  p-value:  0.000000\n  Revenue:  $503.99\n  Result:   PASS\n\nOnly Group C ($4.99) shows a purchase rate significantly\nexceeding its revenue target threshold."
      }
    ]
